---
export const prerender = true;

import RoomLayout from '../../../../layouts/RoomLayout.astro';
import WorkingEditionBanner from '../../../../components/zybach/WorkingEditionBanner.astro';
import ZybachNav from '../../../../components/zybach/ZybachNav.astro';
import ProvenanceBlock from '../../../../components/zybach/ProvenanceBlock.astro';
import { places, SECTION } from '../../../../components/zybach/smith/data';

const base = '/collections/zybach/jedediah-smith';

const parents = [
  { label: 'The Zybach Collection', href: '/collections/zybach/' },
  { label: 'Jedediah Smith and the 1828 Umpqua Memorial', href: `${base}/` },
];
---

<RoomLayout
  room="collections"
  title="Places"
  subtitle="Public named places only — towns, rivers, museums, forts and roadside markers. No map, no pins, no coordinates."
  width="prose"
  parents={parents}
  sourceId="zybach-jedediah-smith-places"
>
  <p slot="before-title" class="kicker">The Zybach Collection · Jedediah Smith</p>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="series:jedediah-smith" hidden></span>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="collection:zybach" hidden></span>

  <ZybachNav current="finding-aid" />
  <WorkingEditionBanner />

  <p class="sm-lede">
    Every other series in this collection has a map. This one does not, and the
    absence is deliberate. This series is about a killing, a burial and a search
    for graves, and a map is the one form in which that material does the harm
    the Library is trying to avoid. What follows instead is a plain list of the
    public places the creator names — the towns, the rivers, the forts, the
    museums and the roadside markers a reader could drive to today.
  </p>
  <p class="sm-note">
    The finest the Library gets about the events of 1828 is “the mouth of the
    Smith River, where it enters the Umpqua” — the creator’s own words on his own
    front page, and the level of detail at which the story has been told in print
    for two centuries. It publishes nothing finer: no coordinate, no township and
    section, no road intersection, no aerial frame, no site map, and no
    photograph captioned as the spot.
  </p>

  <dl class="sm-places">
    {places.map((p) => (
      <div>
        <dt>{p.name}</dt>
        <dd>
          {p.note}
          <span class="sm-places__src">{p.source}</span>
        </dd>
      </div>
    ))}
  </dl>

  <section aria-labelledby="campsites">
    <h2 id="campsites">The 1828 campsites</h2>
    <p class="sm-note">
      The four guidebooks name every camp of the journey and give the 1828 date
      for each — Hogback Ridge, Gans Prairie, the Winchuck, the Rogue, the
      Coquille, Sunset Bay, the Hollering Place, Umpqua Bay and the rest. Those
      names, with their dates and the pages they stand on, are in the
      {' '}<a href={`${base}/chronology/`}>chronology</a> and in the guidebooks
      themselves, printed
      {' '}<a href={`${base}/writings/#guidebooks`}>page by page</a>. They are
      camps on a route, not sites, and the Library treats them as the creator
      does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="homelands">
    <h2 id="homelands">Whose country this was</h2>
    <p class="sm-note">
      The creator’s guidebooks name the peoples whose lands the party crossed, in
      their own subtitles: Hoopa, Yurok and Tolowa in the first; Tolowa, Tututni,
      Nasomah, Miluk, Hanis and Quuiich in the second; Chinook, Kalapuyan,
      Umpqua, Quuiich, Siuslaw and Hanis in the third. His own sketch map, “Oregon
      Coast Native Lands 1828,” sets out the coastal homelands as he understands
      them; it is on the <a href={`${base}/maps/`}>maps page</a>.
    </p>
    <p class="sm-note">
      The people at the mouth of the Umpqua in 1828 are called several things in
      these documents — Kelawatset, Kellywasats, Quuiich, Lower Umpqua — and the
      creator’s letter to the Oregon Travel Information Council argues at length
      about which is right. The Library uses Lower Umpqua (Kelawatset) in its own
      sentences and leaves every source’s spelling alone. The Confederated Tribes
      of Coos, Lower Umpqua and Siuslaw Indians are the tribal government today.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ProvenanceBlock
    items={[
      { label: 'Built by', value: 'America’s School Trust Library, from the creator’s published files' },
      { label: 'Scope', value: 'Public named places only: towns, rivers, forts, museums, quarries and roadside markers' },
      { label: 'Not published', value: 'Any coordinate, survey description, road intersection or map that would locate the 1828 attack, the Kelawatset village or the trappers’ graves' },
      { label: 'The creator’s pages', value: 'orww.org/1828_Umpqua_Memorial/', href: SECTION },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="sm-see-also">
    <a href={`${base}/`}>Series</a> ·
    <a href={`${base}/maps/`}>Maps</a> ·
    <a href={`${base}/chronology/`}>Chronology</a> ·
    <a href={`/correct${Astro.url.pathname}`}>Submit a correction</a>
  </p>
</RoomLayout>

<style>
  .sm-lede { max-width: 64ch; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.68; }
  .sm-note { max-width: 64ch; font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.64; color: #3f3a33; }
  .sm-places { margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem; }
  .sm-places > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 15rem 1fr;
    gap: 0 1.2rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid #ece5d5;
  }
  .sm-places dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--deep-navy, #14243c);
  }
  .sm-places dd { margin: 0; max-width: 62ch; font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .sm-places__src {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.15rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.73rem;
    color: #6b6355;
  }
  .sm-see-also { margin-top: 2rem; font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: 0.84rem; }
  @media (max-width: 42rem) {
    .sm-places > div { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.15rem; }
  }
</style>
